ironment to regulate order to  restrain a multitude of economic  enterprises all of which infringe upon  our economic Liberty or even more  examples and these excesses even extend  to the view of Taxation as well along  with the predictable class warfare  rhetoric in his recent budget speech  this week Americans heard our president  refer to efforts to preserve low tax  rates as quote spending in the tax code  the president described tax relief and  tax deductions as quote tax expenditures  adding that the tax code is quote loaded  up with spending on things like itemized  deductions taxing is now spending this  Washington view of taxation of property  assumes that all wealth belongs to the  government and when the government is  denied access to that some of the well  that constitutes spending in the tax  code this view of property I would  submit the role of government in the  history of abuses I just described is it  odds with the principles enshrined in  the Constitution of the United States  while the founders considered limited  government to be the vanguard of our  Liberty due to the influence of  progresses beginning more than a century  ago many Americans have lost sight of  this simple truth as you will hear today  the founders believed that human nature  was fixed in flawed and instituted a  strong but limited government to protect  natural rights progressives believed  that human nature was malleable and  perfectible and instituted government by  experts and administrators designed to  adapt to contemporary circumstances and  protect collective rights the founders  of this nation saw the protection of  property as the first object of  government in his famous essay on the  topic the father of our Constitution  James Madison described property as  follows quote a man's land or  merchandise or money but also quote he  said a man has property in his opinions  and the free communication of them his  religious opinions and in the profession  and practice dictated by them and in the  safety and liberty of his person in a  word he said as a man is said to have a  right to his property he may be equally  said to have a property in his rights  Madison understood that the genesis of  property rightly understood was  immaterial it was ideas future hope  willingness to risk and a love of  improving nature of turning the  wasteland into a garden and Madison  believed and I quote where an excess of  power prevails property of know so  is duly respected no man is safe in his  opinions his person his faculties or his  possessions put more bluntly he  concluded if there be a government then  which prides itself in maintaining the  inviolability of property yet directly  violates the property which individuals  have such a government is not a pattern  for the United States the pattern of  government for this nation can be found  in the Constitution of the United States  the Constitution given life by the  luminous and passionate declaration of  independence is terse sober and specific  it is not a fluctuating and inconstant  document it is not a collection of  suggestions the purpose of which was to  stimulate debate in the future  progressives tell us that even the  framers themselves could not reach an  agreement in its regard but they did  agree upon it they wrote it down they  signed it and they live by it it's words  are unchanging and unchangeable except  as planned by careful amendment any  citizen aspiring to choose Liberty  should regard the Constitution and the  Declaration as the essence of American  governance and they should be  preoccupied with adherence to its  principles these documents should be on  their mind all the time they should be  the prism through which the light of all  questions of governance pass though we  have sometimes gradually sometimes  radically moved away from this we could  move back to it again the Constitution  was designed to preserve Liberty it  established a just government with  specific enumerated powers checks and  balances all designed to restrain  government excess and the abuse of power  that might intrude upon the property of  the American people while the Bill of  Rights contains specific restraints on  the power of the national government the  pattern of limited government  encompasses the Republic as ordain and  establish and it remains the vanguard of  our liberties today the protections of  property are not only written in the  amendments they are interwoven  throughout the document itself  and enshrine in the principles of  limited government a unique opportunity  to advance our economic Liberty in  particular I would submit can be found  in advancing the constitutional  principle of federalism to confront the  fiscal crisis facing our country it will  not be sufficient to simply reduce  federal government spending we must also  use the resources in the Constitution to  permanently reduce the size and scope of  the federal government by recognizing  the distinction between the powers  granted the federal government and those  reserved to the states into the people  in his first inaugural address President  Reagan reminded the American people that  quote the federal government did not  create the states the states created the  federal government one year later  President Reagan came to Indiana to  address the issue of federalism before a  joint session of our state General  Assembly he used that Hoosier backdrop  to announce a new presidential  commission on federalism which he called  the new phase of the great American  experiment as a start he offered to  return 40 federal programs and the  revenues associated with them back to  the States the Commission and its work  fell flat but President Reagan was right  30 years ago and he's more right today  for his efforts to succeed it would have  required an unprecedented level of  leadership rising at the state and local  level which is exactly what's happening  today perhaps providentially the  leadership and initiative the President  Reagan called for 30 years ago is  emerging in states across the country  consider the courageous stands that  governors in new jersey Wisconsin Ohio  and Indiana are taking on behalf of  fiscal discipline and reform or the  actions taken by States rejecting the  individual mandate in Obamacare and  opposing the imposition of cap and trade  or those responding to inaction by the  federal government on illegal  immigration or traditional marriage in  state after state we find unprecedented  leadership and initiative that is  changing the dynamics of our national  debate with so many states taking a  stand in so many different ways for  their constitutional significance it's  clear that this is no temporary trend in  American  politics we are witnessing the rise of  the states to restore limited government  we not only must take notice of this new  dynamic we must encourage it we must  support those who fight within the  federal system and assist those leaders  at the state level who are leading the  fight from without the states were our  past they were forged from the  wilderness the states hold the promise  of the future and I believe if there's  to be a rebirth of constitutional  governance in America it will come from  the state or not at all these are  challenging times in the life of this  still young nation what might our  forbearers say to us knowing what they  knew and having done what they did I  have no doubt that they would tell us to  channel our passions simply speak the  truth and do what's right slowly and  with resolution to work calmly steadily  and without animus or fear to be like a  rock and the tide to let the water  tumble about us to be firm and unashamed  in our love of country I believe they  would encourage us foremost to cling to  first principles never waver in our  belief in the genius of the American  Founding or the goodness of the American  people and this I know we are doing by  joining us